"""Days 3–4: Compose integration and deployment decisions."""
L(2,'1. Compose describes cooperating services','Read this lab as three services with distinct responsibilities.',
 [('Compose service and project','A service describes how to run one component. A Compose project groups its services, network and volumes. The current CLI is docker compose; a legacy top-level version field is unnecessary.'),('One-shot migration job','A short-lived service changes the schema once and exits. Web workers verify the expected schema instead of all racing to upgrade it.')],
 'The stack has db, migrate and api. PostgreSQL stores durable relational state. The migration job applies the reviewed Week42 schema. Only then should the web API begin accepting work. They share an internal network but have different commands, credentials and lifetimes.\n\nCompose is useful for this owned local stack and for repeatable integration checks. It is not automatically a multi-host scheduler or a managed hosting service. Read one service definition at a time: image/build, command, environment, secret grants, dependencies, network and storage. Generate the ignored local secret files first. The generator never replaces existing files; changing a file is not automatically a database password rotation.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\nc=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())\nprint("Project:",c["name"])\nprint("Services:",sorted(c["services"]))\nprint("Migration command:",c["services"]["migrate"]["command"])',
 'Find which service has a published port and which service runs only once.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\nc=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]\nprint("Published:",[n for n,s in c.items() if "ports" in s])\nprint("Migration restart policy:",c["migrate"]["restart"])',
 'Check that the API and migration job use the same application image.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\ns=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]\nprint("Same artifact:",s["api"]["image"]==s["migrate"]["image"])',
 [('Should every web worker run migrations concurrently?','No','Always','Only on startup','Use one reviewed migration owner/job.'),('Does Compose itself buy or provide a hosting server?','No','Yes','Only if named production','A runtime host and its operations remain separate.')],
 'Treat every component as one long-running web process.','Separate database, one-shot schema work and API service roles.','Different lifetimes and responsibilities deserve explicit service definitions.',shell='python ops.py init-secrets\ndocker compose config --quiet\ndocker compose up --build -d --wait api\n# Open http://127.0.0.1:8042/docs')

L(2,'2. Service names, internal ports and network boundaries','Use the database service name instead of the API container’s localhost.',
 [('Service-name DNS','Compose connects participating services to a network where names such as db resolve to that service. Use its container port, not the API’s published host port.'),('Internal network','The lab isolates backend service networking. Publishing an API host port is a separate explicit choice; the database has no host publication.')],
 'From the API container, DB_HOST=db identifies PostgreSQL. DB_HOST=localhost would try to find PostgreSQL inside the API container itself. The database uses its normal internal port 5432; host port 8042 belongs to the HTTP API and is irrelevant to database traffic.\n\nThe API joins a normal frontend bridge for its published HTTP port and the internal backend for database access. The database and migration job join only the backend. An internal-only bridge is not the API ingress path in this recipe. A network boundary reduces accidental exposure but does not authenticate database callers. The API still needs its own database role and password. Local TLS is explicitly disabled only for this isolated Compose exercise. A managed database over another network needs the provider’s verified TLS configuration and trusted CA details. Do not copy the local disable setting blindly into a hosted deployment.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\nc=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())\na=c["services"]["api"]\nprint("API database hostname:",a["environment"]["DB_HOST"])\nprint("Internal backend:",c["networks"]["backend"]["internal"])\nprint("Database host publication:",c["services"]["db"].get("ports",[]))',
 'Create a fictional internal service address without using the browser’s port.',
 'host="db";port=5432\nprint("Internal destination:",f"{host}:{port}")\nprint("Uses published API port:",port==8042)',
 'Find the local TLS setting and state why it needs review on a managed database.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\ne=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]["api"]["environment"]\nprint("Local exercise sslmode:",e["DB_SSLMODE"])\nprint("Hosted rule:","Use provider-verified TLS, not this local override")',
 [('What does DB_HOST=localhost mean in the API container?','The API container itself','The db service','The public hostname','Use the intended service-name boundary.'),('Does an internal network remove the need for a database password?','No','Yes','Only on the first boot','Networking and authentication are separate controls.')],
 'Use DB_HOST=localhost because both services run on one laptop.','Use DB_HOST=db and the database’s internal port.','Container network namespaces keep their loopback addresses separate.')

L(2,'3. PostgreSQL is a server database, not a renamed SQLite file','Understand the driver, dialect and fresh migration lineage used by this lab.',
 [('psycopg; SQLAlchemy dialect','psycopg is the PostgreSQL driver. postgresql+psycopg selects that driver through SQLAlchemy. SQLite-specific connection arguments and PRAGMA statements do not belong on PostgreSQL connections.'),('Dialect portability and fresh lineage','Boolean defaults, constraints and migrations must work on the target database. Week42 uses its own w42 revisions and an independent database; it does not repurpose Week40/41 files.')],
 'A database URL changes transport and dialect, but it cannot make every SQL assumption portable. This project has a separate PostgreSQL engine path, uses boolean defaults/constraints valid for both supported databases and verifies a known migration head before the API starts.\n\nLocal examples keep disposable SQLite state for fast contract checks. Actual Compose tests execute PostgreSQL migrations, authentication and CRUD. The runtime role cannot create schema objects; the migration role owns that work. Keep this practice database separate from prior weeks. Moving existing production data would require a reviewed data migration, integrity checks and a recovery plan, not just changing a connection string.',
 'from sqlalchemy.schema import CreateTable\nfrom sqlalchemy.dialects import postgresql\nfrom orm_api.models import Note\nsql=str(CreateTable(Note.__table__).compile(dialect=postgresql.dialect()))\nprint("Postgres boolean:","BOOLEAN DEFAULT false" in sql)\nprint("Portable constraint:","completed IN (false,true)" in sql)',
 'Read the Week42 migration head without connecting to any database.',
 'from orm_api.database import HEAD\nprint("Expected schema:",HEAD)',
 'Normalise a fictional provider URL and inspect only its non-secret components.',
 'from orm_api.settings import Settings\ns=Settings.from_env({"DATABASE_URL":"postgresql://learner:fictional-only@db/notes?sslmode=require"})\nprint("Driver:",s.database_url.drivername)\nprint("Database:",s.database_url.database)\nprint("TLS mode:",s.database_url.query["sslmode"])',
 [('Does changing the URL prove every migration is portable?','No','Always','Only with an ORM','The real target dialect and database must be checked.'),('Should Week42 point at your existing Week41 database?','No; use its independent practice store','Always','Only to avoid setup','The lab has its own reviewed schema lineage.')],
 'Apply SQLite PRAGMA settings to every SQLAlchemy engine.','Select engine configuration and migrations for the actual dialect.','An abstraction helps portability but does not erase database-specific behaviour.')

L(2,'4. Grant secrets only to the services that need them','Trace a credential from an ignored host file to a runtime consumer without printing it.',
 [('Compose secret grant','Declaring a secret at the top level does not grant it to every service. Each service lists its allowed secret mounts, usually under /run/secrets.'),('Separate database roles','The bootstrap owner, schema migrator and runtime API use separate identities. The API receives DML privileges, not superuser or schema-creation powers.')],
 'The lab generates four fresh ignored files: bootstrap database password, runtime database password, migration database password and JWT signing secret. The API does not receive the bootstrap owner or migration password. The migration job does not need the bootstrap password either. PostgreSQL creates the restricted roles only when its named volume is first initialised.\n\nChanging a secret file later does not update a role stored inside an existing database. Plan credential rotation as a database/provider operation, update consumers, verify and retire the old value. The examples inspect grant names and test fictional file handling; actual credentials remain outside version control, build context, snippets and browser progress.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\ns=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]\nprint("API grants:",s["api"]["secrets"])\nprint("Migration grants:",s["migrate"]["secrets"])\nprint("API database role:",s["api"]["environment"]["DB_USER"])',
 'Confirm that the API does not receive the owner database secret.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\na=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]["api"]\nprint("Owner secret granted:","owner_db_password" in a["secrets"])',
 'Read an explicitly supplied fictional secret fixture and report only its size.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nfrom tempfile import TemporaryDirectory\nfrom orm_api.settings import read_secret_file\nwith TemporaryDirectory() as d:\n    p=Path(d)/"fixture";p.write_bytes(b"x"*64+b"\\r\\n")\n    print("Normalised bytes:",len(read_secret_file(p)))',
 [('Does a top-level secret declaration grant it to every service?','No','Yes','Only after a restart','Service-level grants are explicit.'),('Does editing a password file rotate an existing PostgreSQL role automatically?','No','Always','Only for named volumes','Update stored role credentials through a reviewed rotation process.')],
 'Give the web process a database superuser password for convenience.','Give the web process only its runtime role and required secret mounts.','Separate identities limit both mistakes and the authority of a compromised component.')

L(2,'5. Healthy dependencies and a successful migration gate','Explain why startup order is not the same as readiness or recovery.',
 [('Healthcheck and service_healthy','A healthcheck evaluates a stated condition. Compose can wait for a dependency to be healthy instead of merely started.'),('service_completed_successfully','The API also waits for the one-shot migration job to exit successfully. A failed schema change must not be hidden by starting incompatible web workers.')],
 'PostgreSQL can be running before it is ready to accept connections. The lab first waits for pg_isready, then executes the migration with its own credentials. The API itself checks a known migration head and later exposes /health/ready, which performs a bounded database query. These gates cover different promises.\n\nStartup dependencies do not guarantee that a dependency stays available. If the database fails after startup, liveness can stay 200 while readiness becomes 503. A container marked unhealthy is not automatically restarted just because it is unhealthy; the supervisor’s restart policy and routing behaviour must be understood separately. Recovery tests stop and restart only the owned CI database and verify useful behaviour returns.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\ns=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]\nfor name,condition in s["api"]["depends_on"].items():print(name,condition["condition"])\nprint("Database probe:",s["db"]["healthcheck"]["test"][0])',
 'Check liveness and readiness independently through the isolated API client.',
 'from lesson_support import practice\nwith practice(seed=False) as (app,c,h):\n    print("Liveness:",c.get("/health").status_code)\n    print("Readiness:",c.get("/health/ready").status_code)',
 'Read the bounded internal readiness probe instead of assuming every HTTP response is healthy.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("healthcheck.py").read_text()\nprint("Checks readiness route:","/health/ready" in text)\nprint("Has explicit timeout:","timeout=2" in text.replace(" ",""))',
 [('Does started always mean ready?','No','Always','Only for databases','Readiness tests the intended capability.'),('Does an unhealthy Docker healthcheck itself force a restart?','No','Always','Only with Python','Understand the supervisor policy; health and restart are separate.')],
 'Wait only for a container to exist, then assume all dependencies work.','Gate startup with meaningful readiness and verify runtime failure/recovery too.','Dependency existence, schema compatibility and ongoing availability are different states.')

L(2,'6. Recreate safely and prove persistence','Verify replacement without confusing a persistent volume with a backup.',
 [('Recreate and named-volume retention','A new API container can use the same PostgreSQL service and named volume. Ordinary compose down leaves named volumes by default; down --volumes explicitly deletes them.'),('Persistence smoke test','Create fictional data, replace the API, authenticate again or reuse a valid token, and confirm the same data remains. Do not infer persistence from a successful restart alone.')],
 'The real container smoke check creates an account and note, recreates the API and retrieves the note again. It then exercises a database outage and restores a dump into a separate database. Each step addresses a distinct recovery question. Use this lab’s own Compose project and fictional data.\n\nFor normal learning, stop services without deleting their named volume. Do not run volume-deleting commands against work you want to keep. The workflow’s final cleanup deletes only the temporary CI runner’s lab volume. A retained volume can still be lost with its host, damaged or accidentally removed, so independent backups and restore drills remain necessary.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\nc=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())\nprint("Named volumes:",list(c["volumes"]))\nprint("Database mount:",c["services"]["db"]["volumes"][0])\nprint("Persistence test present:","--force-recreate" in Path("container_smoke.py").read_text())',
 'Find the independent restore database name in the actual integration check.',
 'from pathlib import Path\ntext=Path("container_smoke.py").read_text()\nprint("Separate restore target:","studyhub_restore" in text)\nprint("Checks note count:","SELECT count(*) FROM notes" in text)',
 'Explain which fictional actions need a backup before proceeding.',
 'actions={"restart api":False,"recreate api":False,"delete only data volume":True,"destructive schema change":True}\nfor action,needs in actions.items():\n    if needs:print(action)',
 [('Does retaining a volume prove you have an off-host backup?','No','Yes','Only with Compose','A separate recovery copy and restore evidence are required.'),('Does down --volumes preserve your named database volume?','No','Always','Only if the API is stopped','It explicitly requests volume removal; normal learning shutdown should omit it.')],
 'Delete all volumes whenever deployment behaves strangely.','Preserve data, inspect the owned service, and recover from a tested plan.','Resetting state can hide a defect and destroy the evidence or data you needed.',shell='docker compose ps\ndocker compose logs --tail 30 api\ndocker compose up -d --no-deps --force-recreate api\ndocker compose stop\n# Keep the named volume. Do not add --volumes for a normal shutdown.')

L(3,'1. Static hosting and an API server solve different jobs','Explain why GitHub Pages can serve Study Hub but cannot run this Python API.',
 [('Static hosting','Serves stored HTML, CSS, JavaScript and other files. A Python source file placed there is not a running server process.'),('ASGI host','Runs an application server such as Uvicorn, routes requests to it and supplies runtime configuration and service lifecycle management.')],
 'Study Hub is a self-contained browser application, so its GitHub Pages deployment is appropriate. Its Python examples are learning content. The Notes API instead needs a Python process, a database connection and a host that runs that process. Adding Dockerfile text to a static site does not make its host execute containers.\n\nKeep the learning website and the practice service as separate deployments with separate data responsibilities. Your browser’s course completion stays in Study Hub’s existing storage/backup/sync. A deployed Notes API stores its own fictional practice notes in PostgreSQL. Cross-origin browser access, HTTPS and account security become explicit integration decisions if a future frontend consumes the API.',
 'components={"Study Hub HTML":"static files in a browser","Notes API":"ASGI process","Practice database":"PostgreSQL service"}\nfor component,owner in components.items():print(component+":",owner)',
 'Classify a CSS file and a FastAPI route by required hosting capability.',
 'requirements={"style.css":"static","POST /v1/notes":"Python application process"}\nfor item,kind in requirements.items():print(item,kind)',
 'Build a separation checklist for browser progress and practice API data.',
 'stores={"course progress":"Study Hub browser backup/sync","practice notes":"API database backup"}\nprint("Separate backup responsibilities:",len(set(stores.values())))',
 [('Will uploading app.py to Pages run FastAPI?','No','Yes','Only with a requirements file','Static hosting does not start an ASGI process.'),('Does the practice API replace your Study Hub progress storage?','No','Always','Only after Docker is installed','They remain separate applications and stores.')],
 'Publish Python files on a static host and expect live API routes.','Choose an ASGI/container runtime for the API and keep static hosting for browser files.','Match the host’s runtime capability to the component you intend to run.')

L(3,'2. PaaS: less server maintenance, explicit platform constraints','Prepare a managed web-service deployment without assuming a free plan or hidden features.',
 [('Platform as a Service','A provider runs a web service while managing more of the host lifecycle. You still own application behaviour, data, configuration and release verification.'),('Root directory and health path','For a repository with several projects, select docs/python-week42 as the service root, use its Dockerfile and configure /health/ready as the readiness path where supported.')],
 'A managed container web service is a reasonable first staging environment when you want to focus on the API. The supplied Render-oriented checklist uses a Docker web service and managed PostgreSQL, but you must verify current runtime, region, storage, migration-job and billing capabilities before choosing a plan. No price or free-tier lifetime is assumed here.\n\nProvider defaults differ. Check its PORT contract, secret configuration, startup timeout, outbound access and health behaviour. Keep credentials in its private runtime configuration. Use a one-off/pre-deploy migration capability only if supported by the chosen plan; otherwise arrange one controlled migration job before routing traffic. Provider convenience does not remove the need to test database restore or application authorisation.',
 'service={"root":"docs/python-week42","runtime":"Docker","health":"/health/ready","database":"managed PostgreSQL"}\nfor key,value in service.items():print(key+":",value)',
 'Record three provider capabilities that must be verified before deploying.',
 'checks=["supported container runtime","controlled migration job","persistent managed database"]\nprint("Required checks:",len(checks))\nprint(checks[1])',
 'Create a fictional budget decision that does not claim a real provider price.',
 'estimated={"web":10,"database":12,"storage":3}\nlimit=30\nprint("Fictional monthly estimate:",sum(estimated.values()))\nprint("Within fictional limit:",sum(estimated.values())<=limit)',
 [('Can this lesson guarantee a provider’s current free plan?','No','Yes','Only for small apps','Check current pricing and capabilities before any purchase.'),('Where should production credentials go?','Private runtime configuration','Dockerfile source','Public lesson notes','Keep values out of images and version control.')],
 'Choose a host because an old tutorial says it is free forever.','Check current capabilities, total cost and operational responsibilities.','A deployment choice needs current provider evidence and your own constraints.')

L(3,'3. VPS: more control means more operating work','Name the responsibilities that stay with the server owner.',
 [('VPS','A virtual private server gives a general-purpose host. You manage operating-system updates, firewall rules, runtime setup, service restarts and recovery.'),('Reverse proxy','Accepts public HTTP/HTTPS and forwards to an application service. It can terminate TLS and apply request limits, but only according to its actual configuration.')],
 'A VPS can run a container stack, but the empty machine is only the beginning. Someone must configure a firewall, private database access, a reverse proxy, certificate renewal, patching, log retention, disk alerts and backups. The database and app on one VPS also share a failure domain.\n\nFor a single-host exercise, bind the app privately and expose only the intended reverse-proxy ports. Do not publish PostgreSQL broadly for convenience. Treat Docker daemon access as powerful host authority, not as a harmless application permission. If you prefer learning API behaviour first, a managed service may reduce initial operating work; choose deliberately rather than assuming that more control is automatically better.',
 'responsibilities=["patch host","limit public ports","renew TLS","back up data","test restore","monitor disk"]\nfor n,item in enumerate(responsibilities,1):print(n,item)',
 'Describe the intended public port surface for a fictional reverse-proxy deployment.',
 'public={80:"HTTP redirect/challenge",443:"HTTPS proxy"}\nprivate={8002:"API",5432:"database"}\nprint("Public:",sorted(public))\nprint("Database public:",5432 in public)',
 'Identify the shared failure domain when app and database share one host.',
 'placement={"api":"host-a","database":"host-a","off-host backup":"store-b"}\nprint("App and database same host:",placement["api"]==placement["database"])\nprint("Backup independent host:",placement["off-host backup"]!=placement["database"])',
 [('Does renting a VPS manage your app’s backups automatically?','No','Yes','Only with Docker','Assign and verify each operational responsibility.'),('Should database port 5432 be broadly public just for convenience?','No','Always','Only for a small database','Use controlled private access and proper authentication.')],
 'Treat a rented machine as a completed production deployment.','Budget time for host, proxy, database and recovery operations.','Control brings explicit work that must have an owner.')

L(3,'4. Serverless: execution limits change the design','Explain why request-based functions are not a drop-in long-running Uvicorn server.',
 [('Function runtime; cold start','A function platform invokes bounded executions and may initialise a new environment for a request. Startup delay and execution limits affect the user experience.'),('Ephemeral filesystem and concurrency','Local files are not durable shared state. Many simultaneous function environments can multiply database connections unless pooling and concurrency are designed.')],
 'A serverless option can fit short, event-driven work. It still needs a compatible deployment/runtime adapter, routing and a database strategy. Uploading the same long-running container command does not by itself satisfy every function platform’s invocation contract.\n\nCheck execution duration, request-body limits, streaming support, cold starts, egress and regional database latency. A low request count does not guarantee a lower total bill once database, storage and operational needs are included. Cap concurrency and connection pressure deliberately. This week compares the shape and trade-offs; it does not claim that the supplied Compose topology is already a function-platform deployment.',
 'environments=20;connections_each=3\nprint("Potential database connections:",environments*connections_each)\nprint("Local files are shared durable storage:",False)',
 'Calculate a fictional connection ceiling under an explicit concurrency cap.',
 'concurrency_cap=8;pool_size=2\nprint("Upper planning bound:",concurrency_cap*pool_size)',
 'Choose which workload better matches bounded function execution.',
 'jobs={"generate one thumbnail":"bounded job","keep a socket open indefinitely":"long-lived service"}\nfor job,shape in jobs.items():print(job+":",shape)',
 [('Is a function environment’s local filesystem a shared database?','No','Yes','Only after one warm request','Use a durable external store.'),('Can concurrency multiply database connection demand?','Yes','Never','Only on a VPS','Account for each process/environment and pool.')],
 'Assume a long-running server command works unchanged on every function host.','Match the provider’s invocation, storage, connection and time-limit contract.','A runtime choice changes application assumptions as well as packaging.')

L(3,'5. Managed database: location, TLS, roles and recovery','Choose a database service by operational needs rather than a connection string alone.',
 [('Managed PostgreSQL','A provider operates database infrastructure, but retention, restore access, region, capacity and failover behaviour depend on the selected service.'),('Least privilege and migration identity','The running API needs specific data privileges. Schema changes need a controlled migration identity; neither should casually use a database superuser.')],
 'Keep the app and database near each other to avoid unnecessary latency and data-transfer cost. Verify TLS and certificate requirements using the provider’s documentation; a connection that succeeds with certificate verification disabled is not the same evidence as a verified secure connection.\n\nAsk how to recover a deleted note, a damaged database and an entire unavailable region. Understand who can run a restore, where backups live, how long they are retained and which versions are supported. The local lab demonstrates restricted runtime and migration roles; reproduce equivalent privileges on the selected managed service rather than assuming its default owner account is appropriate for normal requests.',
 'from pathlib import Path\nimport yaml\ns=yaml.safe_load(Path("compose.yaml").read_text())["services"]\nprint("Runtime:",s["api"]["environment"]["DB_USER"])\nprint("Migrator:",s["migrate"]["environment"]["DB_USER"])\nprint("Same identity:",s["api"]["environment"]["DB_USER"]==s["migrate"]["environment"]["DB_USER"])',
 'Record a provider-verification checklist for recovery.',
 'requirements={"retention verified":False,"restore rehearsed":False,"backup access tested":False}\nprint("Ready to claim recovery:",all(requirements.values()))',
 'Normalise a fictional managed URL while preserving its verified-TLS query.',
 'from orm_api.settings import Settings\ns=Settings.from_env({"DATABASE_URL":"postgresql://demo:fictional@database.example/notes?sslmode=verify-full"})\nprint(s.database_url.drivername)\nprint(s.database_url.query["sslmode"])',
 [('Does managed automatically mean restore has been tested for your app?','No','Yes','Only with backups enabled','Rehearse the relevant restore and validate your data.'),('Should the API routinely use the schema owner account?','No','Always','Only for small requests','Separate runtime and migration authority where the platform permits.')],
 'Use the provider’s owner URL everywhere and never test restore.','Configure the needed runtime role and rehearse recovery independently.','A hosted database is a component with explicit security and recovery contracts.')

L(3,'6. Write a deployment decision record','Choose a first staging plan with explicit reasons, uncertainty and cost limits.',
 [('Decision record','A short artifact stating the goal, options, chosen approach, reasons, trade-offs and verification still needed.'),('Total cost of operation','Include application compute, database, storage, backups, egress, logs, domain and your operating time. Example numbers here are fictional planning inputs, not provider quotations.')],
 'For a first fictional Notes API staging release, a managed Docker web service plus managed PostgreSQL is a reasonable candidate if its current capabilities fit. A VPS offers more control with more host work. A function host can suit bounded tasks but needs a runtime/connection design. Write why your chosen option serves your actual goal.\n\nDo not choose by one advertised compute number. Add the pieces that create your monthly total, set an alert/budget policy and decide what happens when a limit is approached. A written record should identify assumptions still unverified. It is acceptable to defer purchase while learning locally; the course’s public deployment exercise is a checklist you execute only with an account and budget you choose.',
 'import json\nrecord={"goal":"fictional staging Notes API","candidate":"managed Docker service + PostgreSQL","reason":"less initial host maintenance","must_verify":["migration job","verified TLS","restore","current total price"]}\nprint(json.dumps(record,indent=2))',
 'Calculate a fictional total including more than compute.',
 'from decimal import Decimal\nitems={"web":Decimal("10"),"db":Decimal("12"),"backup":Decimal("3"),"logs":Decimal("2")}\nprint("Fictional total:",sum(items.values()))\nprint("Includes database:","db" in items)',
 'Write a release gate that stays closed while provider assumptions remain unchecked.',
 'checks={"runtime":True,"database":True,"restore":False,"cost_limit":True}\nprint("Ready to commit to this host:",all(checks.values()))\nprint("Still missing:",[k for k,v in checks.items() if not v])',
 [('Are the budget numbers in this lesson provider prices?','No; they are fictional inputs','Yes','Only for Render','Check live provider pricing before spending.'),('Should a decision record hide unverified assumptions?','No','Yes','Only for a small app','Record uncertainty so the next action is clear.')],
 'Pick the lowest-looking compute price and ignore data and recovery costs.','Compare the whole service and the work you must operate.','A defensible deployment choice makes constraints and evidence explicit.')
